==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|noughti}}, {{m|enm|naughty|t=evil, immoral, wicked; bungling, ineffectual; in want, needy; evil or hostile person}}{{nb...|naȝti, nauȝti, nauȝty, nawghty, nouthti|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=noughtī|pos=adj|id=MED29942}}</ref> from {{m|enm|nought|t=evil, immoral; of poor quality, worthless; unworthy; inappropriate, unsuitable; impotent, powerless, weak; useless; of an agreement, decree, or obligation: null, void; trivial; diseased}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=nought|pos=adj|id=MED29934}}</ref> + {{m|enm|-ī̆|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming {{glossary|adjective}}s}}.<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=-ī̆|pos=suf|id=MED21577}}</ref> {{m|enm||Nought}} is derived from {{inh|en|ang|nōwiht}}, {{m|ang|nāwiht|t=nothing}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*naiwwiht|t=nothing}}. The English word is analysable as {{suffix|en|naught|y}}.

